ok   the checked-in gen/models files match the emitter (fixture stale otherwise)
ok   scan_dir finds Comment and Post, Post's summary view method and both declarations
ok   write_all writes every model file under gen/models
ok   create and find through the generated classes
ok   column defaults become initial values and reach the database
ok   validations from app/models/post.rb apply to the generated class
ok   belongs_to and has_many associations
ok   call_view_method reaches the scanned summary
ok   to_json of a generated model
ok   decimal, json and date attributes round-trip as Float, Hash and Date
ok   a nullable decimal, json and date left unset load back as nil and are stored as NULL
ok   a NOT NULL json column starts as {} and round-trips a Hash
ok   column_type and decimal_scale answer per column from the schema
ok   the generated query methods: where.not, where with a String and a Range, where_between on a date, or, order by Hash
ok   update_columns writes through the generated to_row: decimal rounded to its scale, json as text
ok   a NOT NULL date: invalid assigned text stays nil and the save fails validation; a valid date round-trips
ok   a NOT NULL date with a default: a fresh record holds the default, invalid text fails validation
ok   update_all rounds a decimal to its scale as save does, and nil stores NULL
ok   scopes from app/models/post.rb run as class methods and chain on a relation
ok   post.comments is the comments' Relation: it chains, counts and orders
ok   comment.post is read once per foreign key, again when the key changes, and after reload
ok   belongs_to :post without optional: validates post_id
ok   post.destroy destroys each of its comments (their callbacks run), then the post
ok   a destroy that raises rolls back the post and every comment and keeps the record persisted
ok   comments_count follows create, destroy and a moved comment; the post's own save leaves it alone
ok   two loaded copies of one comment moved to different posts leave every count right
ok   destroy decrements the post the row points to, not a stale or unsaved in-memory key
ok   a move whose save raises inside the transaction leaves the counts and the row as they were
